# Routes and pages

A workbook becomes a site when the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus) serves
it. Most of the time you don't write routes at all — a docs-style app declares
sections and pages and the runtime renders the navigation for you. When you need an
HTTP endpoint of your own, you declare a **route**.

## Pages come for free

An `app` composition declares its structure, and the runtime turns that into a routed
site — sidebar, clean URLs, deep links — with no front-end code:

app :docs do
  title "Workbooks"
  section "Introduction" do
    page "introduction/what-is-a-workbook"
  end
end

Each `page` is a `.work` file of prose; the runtime server-renders it and wires the
router. You link pages with `[text](/section/page)` and reload-safe deep links just
work. This is the common case — declare the tree, get a site.

## Routes for endpoints

When you need a real HTTP endpoint — an API, a form handler, a webhook — declare a
**`route`** inside a [`server`](/language/placement-and-languages) unit. A route maps
a method-and-path to a handler:

server api :orders do
  route "POST /orders", :create
  route "GET /orders/:id", :show

  def create(req), do: Store.insert(:orders, req.body)
  def show(req), do: Store.get(:orders, req.params.id)
end

The path can carry params (`:id`), and the verb is part of the declaration. The
server unit holds both the routes and their handlers, side by side.

## Guarding a route

Routes default to whatever the workbook's access posture is; you mark the exceptions.
A route can be made **`public`** (no auth) or **`protect`**ed (auth required), so the
guard is visible in the source next to the route it covers rather than buried in
middleware. The same guards apply whether a request arrives with a session cookie or
a minted token — the token carries scopes and is subject to the same per-route check.

## One document, browser to endpoint

A page renders prose, a [`client`](/language/placement-and-languages) island runs in
the browser, a `server` route answers HTTP, and a [resource](/data/resources-and-the-store)
stores the rows behind it — all in the same tree, all read by the
[one parser](/language/block-grammar). The site isn't a separate app wired to the
workbook; the workbook *is* the site. See how one runtime hosts more than one of them
in [One nexus, many sites](/blog/the-nexus-serves-many-sites), and how a site renders
itself in [Site mode & llms.txt](/serving/site-mode-and-llms-txt).
